<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristobulus of Chalcis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristobulus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> was a Herodian prince, a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod of Chalcis</a>. In the mid-50s CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> made him king of Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and from 57 he also ruled Chalcis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He married Salome, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>, and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in its eastern wars, including the annexation of Commagene in 72 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristobulus-of-chalcis.jpg" alt="Aristobulus of Chalcis" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristoboulos_king_of_Lesser_Armenia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aristobulus was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod of Chalcis</a> and his first wife Mariamne. His father, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in Iturea, was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> through Aristobulus IV, and his mother a granddaughter of Herod the Great through her mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, so he descended from Herod on both sides. He married his first cousin Salome after the death of her first husband, Philip the Tetrarch, and they had three sons, Herod, Agrippa and Aristobulus. Three coins bearing portraits of him and Salome have been found.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He did not succeed his father directly. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod of Chalcis</a> died in 48 CE, the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> gave the realm to Aristobulus's cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Herod Agrippa II</a>, but only as a tetrarchy. In 53 Agrippa II gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in exchange for the title of king and the lands once ruled by Philip the Tetrarch and Lysanias. Aristobulus received the tetrarchy of Chalcis in 57 and held it, according to his coins, until 92, when the territory became part of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>He has been identified with the Aristobulus whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> made king of Armenia Minor in 55, who fought on the Roman side in the Roman–Parthian War of 58 to 63 and was rewarded with a small part of Greater Armenia. That king lost Armenia Minor in 72. He is also thought to be the Aristobulus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a> who aided Lucius Caesennius Paetus, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, against Antiochus of Commagene, and who was then given a new kingdom, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinnasrin">Chalcis ad Belum</a>, the modern Qinnasrin in northern Syria. If all these are one man, he ruled at different times the Iturean Chalcis, Armenia Minor and Chalcis ad Belum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aristobulus of Chalcis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 57 Chalcis was ruled by <b>Aristobulus</b>, son of Herod of Chalcis and husband of Salome, who also held Lesser Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-chalcis">Herodian Kingdom of Chalcis</a></li><li><blockquote>Its last king, <b>Aristobulus</b>, a great-grandson of Herod, ruled until c. 71 CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lesser-armenia">Lesser Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
